<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bad Ems</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bad Ems is a spa town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>. Its warm springs made it one of the most fashionable resorts of 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, visited by royalty. It is best known for the Ems Dispatch of July 1870, Bismarck's edited report of King William I's meeting there with the French ambassador, which helped provoke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bad-ems.jpg" alt="Bad Ems" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julian Lupyan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buildings_in_Bad_Ems,_Germany.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bad Ems lies on both banks of the River Lahn, the natural border between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westerwald">Westerwald</a>, two parts of the Rhenish Slate Mountains, within the Nassau Nature Reserve. It is the administrative seat of the Rhein-Lahn district and stands on a cluster of mineral hot springs. The waters are high in sodium bicarbonate and range in temperature from 27 to 57 °C. Natural Ems salt is produced from them and is sold for drinking and inhalation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Roman times a castrum was built at Bad Ems as part of the Upper Germanic Limes, and distinct traces of the border remain in the woods around the town. Bad Ems was first mentioned in official documents in 880 and received its town charter in 1324. The Counts of Nassau and Katzenelnbogen rebuilt the bath. In the 17th and 18th centuries it was considered one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s best-known bathing resorts, and in the 19th century it welcomed visitors from all over the world and became the summer residence of European monarchs and artists, among them Kaiser Wilhelm I, Tsars Nicholas I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II of Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-dostoevsky">Fyodor Dostoevsky</a>.</p><p>Metal ores, mainly lead, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, zinc and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, were mined here from Roman times, with underground mining first mentioned in 1158 and continuing, with long interruptions, until 1945. In 1870 the town, then part of Prussian Hesse-Nassau, became known as the place where the Ems Dispatch originated, instigating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. In 1876 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> signed the Ems Edict, banning the use of the Ukrainian language, in the Haus Vier Türme.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bad Ems has around 9,000 inhabitants. In 2021 it became part of the transnational UNESCO World Heritage Site Great Spa Towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, because of its hot springs and its 18th to 20th century architecture. Its former mine, known as Mercur, has been a museum since 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bad Ems” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spa towns such as Wiesbaden, <b>Bad Ems</b> and Schlangenbad attracted international visitors, and the Rheingau's vineyards and the Lahn's iron mines supported the economy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-nassau">Duchy of Nassau</a></li></ul></section>
